<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.ModelingGuidelines
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:cap="org.blockml.bml.capability"
  xmlns:constraint="org.blockml.bml.constraint"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <name>ModelingGuidelines</name>
  <type>doc:ModelingGuidelines</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A guide to modeling patterns</is>

  <documentation>
    Inheritance (baseType):
    • Single inheritance — each Block has exactly one baseType (mandatory except org.blockml.bml.core.Block)
    • Use for branch membership (ValueType, Language, domain base) and shared member fragments
    • Framework branches inherit via org.blockml.bml.core.ValueType, Language, or domain bases
    • final on Block or member prevents further override → BML_FINAL_BASE_TYPE / BML_FINAL_MEMBER_OVERRIDE

    Contracts:
    • Reusable partial Block definitions — not pure interfaces; may include default implementations
    • Use when multiple Blocks share a member fragment without sharing a common baseType ancestor
    • Declared on root block only via contracts aggregation — direct Block references
    • Merge order: baseType chain → contracts (declaration order) → local declarations
    • Contract may supply partial members (e.g. id type only); concrete Block completes defaults,
      constraints, and documentation
    • subBlocks and compositions on contract types are ignored

    Capabilities:
    • All behaviour — init, get/set, render, domain operations — express as Capabilities
    • Prefer PseudoCode or natural language implementations for semantic descriptions
    • Use Inbound/Outbound/Transformation kinds to describe intent, not compiler behaviour
    • Public surface of a Block is Capabilities — Properties are internal configuration

    Constraints:
    • Declare on Properties via constraints aggregation — Constraint Block instances
    • Generic binding is automatic: Min/Max bind T from property type; MinItems/MaxItems from
      collection element type; MinLength/MaxLength from Text element type
    • Use for validation rules expressible as reusable Constraint blocks (Min, Max, Pattern, …)
    • Suggestions (optional Block instances of property type) guide editors — not validation

    General: keep idea stable; put cross-links in references; prefer explicit baseType over
    implicit defaults; document partial members in contracts clearly.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see file="00_bom_core.md" relation="DeepDive">
      Contracts, inheritance, Properties, Capabilities — core modeling rules.
    </see>
    <see file="16_framework_base_types.md" relation="AuthoringGuide">
      abstract, final, subTypes, languages, contracts, genericTypes modifiers.
    </see>
    <see file="17_type_system_bml.md" relation="AuthoringGuide">
      Contracts section; validation rules by branch.
    </see>
    <see file="18_constraints_and_suggestions.md" relation="AuthoringGuide">
      Property constraints and editor suggestions.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Capability" relation="Extends">
      Capability branch — behaviour as the public semantic surface.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Constraint" relation="Extends">
      Constraint branch — reusable validation rules on Properties.
    </see>
    <see type="cap:Inbound" relation="Defines">
      Inbound capability — when external input is received.
    </see>
    <see type="constraint:Min" relation="SeeAlso">
      Example scalar constraint — binds T from enclosing property type.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:PseudoCode" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — why pseudocode exists for semantic descriptions.
    </see>
  </references>

</block>
